# STUDIO environment. Copy to `.env` to use; `.env` is gitignored and must
# stay that way. Every name here is read in exactly one place:
# `src/platform/harness/connection.ts` -- and only outside Tauri, in a plain
# browser under `vite dev` (or in vitest).

# DEVELOPMENT ONLY. The packaged app does not read either variable: Rust
# starts the harness itself, resolves its address from the supervisor, and
# attaches the credential in `service_fetch`. Nothing set here reaches a
# build -- the read sits behind `import.meta.env.DEV`, so it is dead code in
# any production build and the value cannot be baked into the bundle.

# What they buy, stated honestly: the harness serves no CORS headers, and a
# `vite dev` page is cross-origin to a loopback harness, so its requests die
# at the preflight even with both values set. The reads' working consumer is
# vitest, where the stubbed `fetch` never meets a preflight. A plain browser
# outside Tauri for real is served by Studio's own listener (ADR 0050) and
# needs neither variable.

# Where a hand-started harness is listening. The harness binds 127.0.0.1 on a
# port chosen at launch and prints it; there is no default, so read it from
# the launch output rather than guessing.
VITE_HARNESS_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4319

# The bearer credential minted by whoever launched the harness, sent as
# `Authorization: Bearer <value>` by the browser build only. It is valid for
# that one launch and no longer, so it changes every restart.
#
# Do not commit a real value, and do not set it while producing any artifact:
# `import.meta.env` is inlined at build time, and the only thing standing
# between a value present during a build and that value in the shipped bundle
# is the dev gate described above.
VITE_HARNESS_TOKEN=
